<item>
            <title>[LU-20482] Secondary-NID renumbering on live peer trips BAD_CONFIG guard and hangs I/O</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20482</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h4&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Summary%3A&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Summary:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When a multi-rail peer&apos;s secondary NID is renumbered while&lt;br/&gt;
its primary NID remains reachable, LNetAddPeer() builds a temporary&lt;br/&gt;
peer for the new NID and relies on discovery to merge it into the&lt;br/&gt;
existing peer. In lnet_peer_add_nid() the merge collides with a safety&lt;br/&gt;
check when the existing peer has LNET_PEER_LOCK_PRIMARY set&lt;br/&gt;
with more than one NI. Both peers are marked LNET_PEER_BAD_CONFIG and&lt;br/&gt;
the client hangs on all I/O to that server.&lt;br/&gt;
&#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;a name=&quot;Scenario%3A&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Scenario:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Server peer state on the client transitions:&lt;br/&gt;
&#160;&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID, OLD_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt; --&amp;gt; &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID, NEW_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;&#160;&lt;br/&gt;
&#160;--&amp;gt; means the server rebooted directly. No lnetctl peer del and no&lt;br/&gt;
client-side unmount happens in between.&lt;br/&gt;
On reconnection the client learns the new NID list and&lt;br/&gt;
calls LNetAddPeer(&lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID, NEW_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;). TCP_NID is the primary and remains&lt;br/&gt;
reachable; OLD_NID was a secondary on the same peer and has been replaced&lt;br/&gt;
by NEW_NID.&lt;br/&gt;
&#160;&lt;br/&gt;
LNetAddPeer() builds a fresh peer lp with lp_primary_nid = NEW_NID&lt;br/&gt;
and lp_merge_primary_nid = TCP_NID, and kicks discovery on NEW_NID.&lt;br/&gt;
Discovery&apos;s ping reply lists &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID, NEW_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;. lnet_peer_merge_data(lp, pbuf)&lt;br/&gt;
computes addnis = &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt; and calls lnet_peer_add_nid(lp, TCP_NID, ...).&lt;br/&gt;
This will fail because TCP_NID already has it&apos;s peer &lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;TCP_NID, OLD_NID&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;br/&gt;
&#160;&lt;br/&gt;
Would like to know if this is a valid concern, and if so if the proposed fix makes sense.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
